#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d911831-d43b-5217-aa13-6c86953402f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363790} {:answer/id #uuid "616927ef-758e-59b3-9a1d-7e0eda25d3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363791} {:answer/id #uuid "e43a7b97-5207-55f0-9f7e-1414c06eac3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363792} {:answer/id #uuid "1a71692f-fb6b-5951-9492-a99105e72e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363793} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee9afda-ada5-5ac7-8ac3-58b336211770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a71692f-fb6b-5951-9492-a99105e72e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363793} :answer-field/id #uuid "56727957-d5b4-5b1b-b866-033cd2dec2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02b892c4-044a-53a5-940f-9a5ebb949669" :question/math-academy-id "q-84607" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
